lklfuse: owner= for new files, and the image opened close-on-exec

linuxfs shows every file as the Mac user's (libfuse's uid=/gid=), so files it creates need on-disk ids
of their own: owner=root gives new files, directories, symlinks and device nodes the filesystem root's
uid and gid; owner=UID:GID gives those ids. create() takes O_EXCL first so that only a file it makes is
chowned, retrying when the name vanishes in between. A filesystem that cannot store the ids keeps its own, without error.

The image and lock files are opened O_CLOEXEC, so libfuse's mount helper (macFUSE's mount_macfuse)
does not inherit a descriptor for the device. The lock file is opened O_NOFOLLOW. A file named /dev/fd/N (linuxfs-open passes the
helper's descriptor as fd 3) is used as that descriptor, made close-on-exec too.

